@* Components/AppStatusCard.razor — shared status card (CLAUDE.md §3.1). Used
   by BOTH card rows on Screen A (the 3-card row under the sync-action
   buttons, and the 4-card "صحة خط المزامنة" row below it) — one component,
   not two, since both rows render the identical card anatomy with only the
   content differing. Confirmed against the Figma reference before coding:
     1. Header row — title text one side, a small dark rounded icon badge
        (per-card icon, passed in, never hardcoded) on the other.
     2. A horizontal divider.
     3. Ring (percentage inside) + status label + optional meta rows below.

   Container: Bordered (new parameter, default true) controls whether this
   card supplies its own .app-panel border/background. Two real call sites,
   two different answers, both confirmed against the Figma reference:
     - The 4-card "صحة خط المزامنة" row: Bordered="true" (default) — each
       card keeps its own individually-bordered/gapped look, unchanged from
       how this component was originally built.
     - The 3-card CBT row: Bordered="false" — that row is now nested inside
       .app-sync-block (components.css), ONE shared .app-panel spanning both
       the sync-action banners above it AND this card row, separated by
       divider LINES (border-inline-end/border-block-end) rather than
       per-card borders. See components.css's "Sync-actions block" section
       for the full reasoning — this replaced an earlier, incorrect
       two-separate-panels reading of the same reference.
   Below 600px both call sites revert to individually-boxed stacked cards
   regardless of Bordered (components.css's mobile block forces the panel
   look back on), matching TaskOne_Mobile.png. Only NEW classes here are
   this component's own internal layout (header/divider/body/meta), which is
   the reusable pattern itself (this component IS the shared visual pattern,
   per §3.1's rule of thumb), so it lives in styles/components.css, not a
   .razor.css.

   ============================================================================
   STATUS COLOR MAPPING — confirmed by comparing ring colors across cards,
   not assumed from status-label text alone
   ============================================================================
   The Figma reference uses the SAME label text ("تمت") on two different
   cards with two visibly DIFFERENT ring colors: 80% dark-navy/teal, and a
   separate 100% card in green. That rules out deriving color purely from
   StatusLabel string-matching — StatusType and StatusLabel are kept as two
   independent parameters specifically so a consumer can pair e.g.
   StatusType="AppStatusType.Info" with StatusLabel="تمت" on one card and
   StatusType="AppStatusType.Success" with StatusLabel="تمت" (or "ناجح") on
   another, exactly matching what the reference actually shows.
   Mapping used (tokens.css, new --status-* tokens):
     Success (100%, "تمت"/"ناجح")            → green  (--status-color-success)
     Info    (80%/"تمت", 62%/"قيد التشغيل")   → dark navy/teal (--status-color-info)
     Warning (74%, "جزئي")                    → orange (--status-color-warning)
     Neutral (0%, "لا يوجد")                  → gray   (--status-color-neutral)
   FLAGGED, not pixel-sampled: the exact ring hues above are read visually off
   the pasted screenshot (no Figma color/hex export available in this
   conversation), so --status-color-info in particular is a best-match to an
   EXISTING token (--color-gray-39, already used for sidebar/icon dark
   accents) rather than a new value confirmed against a real swatch — revisit
   if exact Figma hex values surface later.

   ============================================================================
   MudProgressCircular — verified against installed MudBlazor 8.7.0
   ============================================================================
   Checked MudBlazor.xml directly (not assumed): Value/Min/Max (doubles,
   Min/Max default 0/100 — a plain int percent maps straight onto Value with
   no extra scaling), Color (theme enum), StrokeWidth, ChildContent
   ("RenderFragment for rendering custom content" — MudBlazor's own
   documented mechanism for the percentage-in-ring pattern used here, not a
   custom absolute-positioned span). Color is deliberately left at
   Color.Default (no MudBlazor theme-color class fighting our own tokens) —
   the actual ring stroke color is themed entirely via mudblazor-overrides.css
   against MudProgressCircular's real rendered SVG classes, confirmed via a
   built/rendered DOM check (see that file's header comment for the exact
   structure found), per CLAUDE.md rule 1.

   ============================================================================
   MetaRows API — plain List<(string Label, string Value)>, not RenderFragment
   ============================================================================
   Considered a RenderFragment for the meta section instead, but every card
   in the reference — all 7 of them — needs nothing more than plain
   "label: value" text pairs; none show an icon, link, or any richer markup
   inside a meta row. A RenderFragment would just mean every call site
   hand-writes the same <p><span>label</span><span dir="ltr">value</span></p>
   markup instead of a plain tuple list, for no actual flexibility gained
   today (CLAUDE.md: don't build for hypothetical future needs). Kept as a
   tuple list; revisit only if a future meta row genuinely needs non-text
   content. Defaults to an empty list (not null) — 3 of the 7 real cards
   (الإقرارات/التوزيع/المعالجة المركزية) have NO meta rows at all in the
   reference, just header+ring+status, so the empty-list case is a real,
   exercised path, not just a defensive default.

   Value is ALWAYS rendered in its own dir="ltr" span, separate from Label —
   not one combined "label: value" string. A combined string would rely on
   the browser's bidi algorithm to correctly re-order an embedded numeral run
   from context alone, which does work when a strong-RTL label sits directly
   in the same text node, but silently breaks for a value with no adjoining
   label in that line (e.g. a bare date on its own row) — exactly the bug
   CLAUDE.md §2 calls out. Splitting Label/Value into two spans and pinning
   dir="ltr" on the value one is the safe, uniform fix for both cases at
   once, not just the risky one.

   ============================================================================
   Accessibility (CLAUDE.md §6, per your explicit scoping for this card)
   ============================================================================
   - The percentage is already real visible text inside the ring
     (MudProgressCircular's ChildContent), not color-only — kept.
   - The ring's OUTER wrapper carries role="img" + a full aria-label
     ("الحالة: <StatusLabel> <percent>%") so a screen reader gets one
     coherent description instead of just announcing the bare "62%" text
     node; the MudProgressCircular itself and its ChildContent are marked
     aria-hidden so the composed label isn't announced twice.
   - The adjacent status-label text (already present in the Figma, kept
     as-is) continues to pair a real status WORD with the ring's color, for
     every card — never a colored ring alone.
   - No extra status icon is added next to StatusLabel beyond what the
     reference shows: you scoped this card's a11y ask to the ring's
     aria-label + keeping the existing text pairing, not to inventing a new
     icon the Figma reference doesn't have. *@

<div class="@RootClass">
    <div class="app-status-card__header">
        <span class="app-status-card__icon-badge" aria-hidden="true">
            <MudIcon Icon="@Icon" Color="Color.Inherit" Size="Size.Medium" />
        </span>
        <span class="app-status-card__title">@Title</span>
        @* Icon bumped Size.Small -> Size.Large this pass, following on from
           --status-card-badge-size growing 32px -> 56px (tokens.css,
           visually recalibrated against the Figma reference): Size.Small
           (~20px, MudBlazor's shipped CSS) inside a 56px circle would leave
           the glyph looking lost/undersized relative to the old 32px-circle/
           20px-icon ratio (~62%). Size.Large (~36px) restores a comparable
           ~64% fill ratio — not itself pixel-measured against the
           reference, a proportion-preserving follow-on from the badge
           change, flagged as such rather than presented as independently
           traced. *@
        
    </div>

    <hr class="app-status-card__divider" />

    <div class="app-status-card__body">
        @* Layout revised this pass: status label + ring now share one row
           (previously: ring in its own column spanning the full body
           height, meta stacked beside it). Re-confirmed against
           TaskOne_from_figma_design.png by pixel position, not assumed: on
           every one of the 7 real cards the ring sits at the visual LEFT
           edge and the status label at the visual RIGHT, same row. In an
           RTL flex row the first DOM child renders at the right
           (inline-start), so the status label is written FIRST and the
           ring SECOND to land ring-left/label-right — the opposite of what
           "ring first, it's visually left-most" would naively suggest. *@
        <div class="app-status-card__status-row">
            <p class="app-status-card__status-label">
                <span>الحالة:</span> <span>@StatusLabel</span>
            </p>

            <div class="app-status-card__ring" role="img" aria-label="@AccessibleRingLabel">
                @* Size.Large left UNCHANGED — measured against the reference
                   this pass (canvas pixel-scan, TaskOne_from_figma_design.png,
                   1920px width): reference ring outer diameter ~54px vs this
                   component's rendered ~56px at the same viewport width, close
                   enough that no change is warranted. *@
                <MudProgressCircular Value="@ProgressPercent"
                                      Color="Color.Default"
                                      Size="Size.Large"
                                      Class="@RingClass"
                                      UserAttributes="@_ringHiddenAttributes">
                    <span class="app-status-card__ring-value" aria-hidden="true">@ProgressPercent%</span>
                </MudProgressCircular>
            </div>
        </div>

        @* Remaining MetaRows now render as a 2-column grid below the status
           row (previously: one per line). Guarded by Count > 0 so the 3 of
           7 real cards with no MetaRows don't emit an empty grid container. *@
        @if (MetaRows.Count > 0)
        {
            <div class="app-status-card__meta">
                @foreach (var row in MetaRows)
                {
                    <p class="app-status-card__meta-row">
                        @* Empty Label (e.g. the unlabeled time half of a
                           split date/time pair) must not print a dangling
                           ":" with nothing before it. *@
                        @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(row.Label))
                        {
                            <span class="app-status-card__meta-label">@row.Label:</span>
                        }
                        <span class="app-status-card__meta-value" dir="ltr">@row.Value</span>
                    </p>
                }
            </div>
        }
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// e.g. Icons.Material.Filled.People — one per card, passed in by the
    /// consumer, never hardcoded here (same convention as AppButton.Icon).
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Icon { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter] public int ProgressPercent { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public AppStatusType StatusType { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string StatusLabel { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// See the MetaRows API note above for why this is a plain tuple list,
    /// not a RenderFragment. Empty by default — genuinely exercised: 3 of
    /// the 7 real cards on this screen pass none.
    [Parameter] public List<(string Label, string Value)> MetaRows { get; set; } = new();

    /// Default true: this card supplies its own .app-panel border/background
    /// (the 4-card "صحة خط المزامنة" row). Pass false when the card is
    /// embedded inside a shared parent panel that already draws the border
    /// and handles separation via divider lines instead (the 3-card CBT
    /// row inside .app-sync-block) — see the container comment above.
    [Parameter] public bool Bordered { get; set; } = true;

    private string RootClass => Bordered ? "app-panel app-status-card" : "app-status-card";

    private static readonly Dictionary<string, object> _ringHiddenAttributes = new() { ["aria-hidden"] = "true" };

    // The status-LABEL text is deliberately NOT color-varied by StatusType —
    // see tokens.css's --status-card-color-status-label note: three of the
    // four raw --status-color-* hues fail 4.5:1 as text. Only the ring below
    // carries the per-status color.

    // Named "-circular" (not just "app-status-card__ring", already used by
    // the wrapping role="img" div above) so the two never collide in CSS.
    private string RingClass => StatusType switch
    {
        AppStatusType.Success => "app-status-card__ring-circular app-status-card__ring-circular--success",
        AppStatusType.Warning => "app-status-card__ring-circular app-status-card__ring-circular--warning",
        AppStatusType.Info => "app-status-card__ring-circular app-status-card__ring-circular--info",
        _ => "app-status-card__ring-circular app-status-card__ring-circular--neutral",
    };

    private string AccessibleRingLabel => $"الحالة: {StatusLabel} {ProgressPercent}%";
}
